content.text:
Done. `addHttpMethod` no longer silently redefines the body semantics of an already-supported method.

**The fix** — `fastify.js:826` (`addHttpMethod`):

```js
function addHttpMethod (method, { hasBody = false, override = false } = {}) {
  ...
  const supportedMethods = this[kSupportedHTTPMethods]
  const isSupported = supportedMethods.bodyless.has(method) || supportedMethods.bodywith.has(method)

  if (isSupported && supportedMethods.bodywith.has(method) !== (hasBody === true) && override !== true) {
    throw new FST_ERR_ROUTE_METHOD_ALREADY_SUPPORTED(method)
  }
  ...
}
```

Behavior now:
- re-adding a method with the same body support is a no-op (plugins that call it twice, and the existing `addHttpMethod('TRACE')` style calls, keep working — verified);
- changing body support of a standard method or of one another plugin registered throws `FST_ERR_ROUTE_METHOD_ALREADY_SUPPORTED`;
- `{ override: true }` is the explicit opt-in that preserves the old behavior.

The shorthand-decorator branch is untouched, so the decorator is still created per encapsulation scope even when the method is already in the supported set.

**Contract files updated in the same change** (per COMPLEX.md's three-way error contract): new code in `lib/errors.js`, `types/errors.d.ts`, and the TOC + table in `docs/Reference/Errors.md`; `types/instance.d.ts` gained the `override` option (`hasBody` also made optional, matching the runtime default); `docs/Reference/Server.md` replaces the "overrides existing methods" warning with the new rule and an example.

**Partners checked:** `lib/route.js` (45% co-change with `fastify.js`) — opened, no change needed: it only reads `kSupportedHTTPMethods` in `normalizeAndValidateMethod`/`validateSchemaBodyOption`. `lib/hooks.js`/`types/hooks.d.ts` are unaffected.

**Tests:** added three cases to `test/http-methods/custom-http-methods.test.js` plus the error-table case in `test/internals/errors.test.js` (count 94 → 95) and a type expectation in `test/types/instance.tst.ts`. I ran only `test/http-methods/custom-http-methods.test.js` (8/8 pass) per your one-file limit — `test/internals/errors.test.js`, the type tests, and the lifecycle suites COMPLEX.md recommends for `fastify.js` were not run; lint on the changed files needed approval, so it was skipped too.

One caveat worth your call: turning a previously-silent override into a throw is a behavior break for anyone relying on it today, so this is semver-major material even though `override: true` gives them a one-line migration.

